<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cave of the Patriarchs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cave of Machpelah, Ibrahimi Mosque, Sanctuary of Abraham</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cave of the Patriarchs, called Machpelah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and the Ibrahimi Mosque by Muslims, is a series of caves in the Old City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. According to Genesis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> bought the cave and its field from Ephron the Hittite as a burial place for Sarah; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> hold it holy as the tomb of the patriarchs and their wives. A large enclosure built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> stands over it. A Byzantine basilica was later built there, and after the Muslim conquest the building became a mosque. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967 it has been divided between a mosque and a synagogue. In February 1994 Baruch Goldstein killed 29 Palestinian Muslims there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cave-of-the-patriarchs.jpg" alt="Cave of the Patriarchs" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Utilisateur:Djampa - Djampa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hebron_Cave_of_the_Patriarchs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies in the heart of the Old City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebron">Hebron</a>, 30 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> name Me'arat haMakhpela is of uncertain meaning, perhaps cave of the double. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> records two explanations: two chambers, one behind or above the other, or a cave doubled in holding couples, the four couples being Adam and Eve, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and Sarah, Isaac and Rebecca, and Jacob and Leah. The stone enclosure holds four cenotaphs, each in its own octagonal room. The caves beneath are generally not accessible, and only two entrances are known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to Genesis 23, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> for a burial site for Sarah and, through Ephron the Hittite, bought the cave and its field for four hundred shekels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. Three of the six real estate contracts found in the Ugaritic texts were for 400 silver shekels, and their terms parallel the biblical sale. Most historians believe the Abraham, Isaac and Jacob narrative to be primarily mythological. Between 31 and 4 BCE Herod the Great built the large rectangular enclosure, the only fully surviving Herodian structure of its period.</p><p>In Byzantine times a basilica was built at the southeastern end, and the Pilgrim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, around 333, described a monument in which lie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a>, Isaac, Jacob, Sarah, Rebecca and Leah. In 637 the area came under Muslim control and the building was rebuilt as a roofed mosque, with two small synagogues permitted. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> captured it in 1100 it became a church and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were banned from the synagogues. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> reconverted it into a mosque in 1188 and added minarets, two of which survive. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, Jews were allowed only as close as the seventh step of a staircase.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> all hold the site holy as the burial place of the patriarchs and matriarchs. Muslims believe that Muhammad stopped at the tomb on his night journey from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The building is divided between a mosque and a synagogue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cave of the Patriarchs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
